    // Assume we have 4 bytes that contain valid data (e.g., from I/O)
    let raw = [0x01u8, 0x02u8, 0x03u8, 0x04u8];
    let mut maybe = MaybeUninit::<[u8; 4]>::uninit();
    // SAFELY copy the raw bytes into an initialized array
    unsafe { ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(raw.as_ptr(), maybe.as_mut_ptr(), 4) };
    // Now we can safely treat it as an array of u8s
    let arr = maybe.assume_init();
    println!("{:#?}", arr);
}
```
- The copy guarantees every byte has a defined value before we ever read them.
- After copying we call `.assume_init()` which tells the compiler we have fully initialized the memory region.
- At this point all operations on `arr` are defined because every bit was set by our explicit copy.

### Step 2 – Using transmute safely (if you really need it)
If you must reinterpret raw bytes as another type and you know they are fully defined (for example after reading from a trusted source), you can do:
```rust
